<main id="main">
<section class="sub-hero"><div class="sub-hero-ph" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/assets/img/photos/winter-house-icicles.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/winter-house-icicles-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/winter-house-icicles.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap sub-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/free-tools/">Free tools</a></li><li aria-current="page">Roof Snow Load Calculator</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Free calculator</p><h1>Roof Snow Load Calculator for Minnesota Homes and Buildings</h1><p class="lede">Snow depth alone says little — weight is what stresses a roof. This roof snow load calculator converts depth, snow type and ice into pounds per square foot.</p></div></section>
<section class="sec tool-sec"><div class="wrap">
<div class="tool" data-tool="snow">
  <div class="tool-head"><span>Roof snow load calculator</span><i>Pounds per square foot</i></div>
  <div class="tool-body">
    <div class="tool-in">
      <div class="row2">
        <div><label for="s-d">Snow depth on the roof (inches)</label><input id="s-d" name="depth" inputmode="decimal" value="22"></div>
        <div><label for="s-t">Type of snow</label><select id="s-t" name="dens"><option value="7">Fresh, light powder (~7 lb/ft³)</option><option value="15" selected>Settled snow (~15 lb/ft³)</option><option value="25">Wet or packed snow (~25 lb/ft³)</option></select></div>
      </div>
      <div class="row2">
        <div><label for="s-i">Ice layer thickness (inches)</label><input id="s-i" name="ice" inputmode="decimal" value="1"><span class="hint">Ice weighs about 57 lb/ft³.</span></div>
        <div><label for="s-a">Roof area (sq ft, optional)</label><input id="s-a" name="area" inputmode="decimal" value="1800"></div>
      </div>
      <div><label for="s-r">Reference roof snow load (psf)</label><select id="s-r" name="ref"><option value="20">20 psf</option><option value="30">30 psf</option><option value="35" selected>35 psf</option><option value="40">40 psf</option><option value="50">50 psf</option></select><span class="hint">Use the design load from your building’s plans or a structural engineer if you have it. These options are examples only.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tool-out" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="out-big"><small>Estimated roof load</small><b data-o="psf">0 psf</b></div>
      <div class="meter" aria-hidden="true"><span data-o="bar"></span></div>
      <div class="out-rows">
        <div><span>Snow layer</span><b data-o="snowpsf">0 psf</b></div>
        <div><span>Ice layer</span><b data-o="icepsf">0 psf</b></div>
        <div><span>Share of reference load</span><b data-o="share">0%</b></div>
        <div><span>Total weight on the roof</span><b data-o="total">—</b></div>
      </div>
      <p class="out-msg" data-o="advice"></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<p class="tool-note">For planning only. Results depend on the figures you enter and your policy’s terms. They are not a quote, an appraisal or legal advice.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>Why Minnesota needs a roof snow load calculator</h2>
<p>Minnesota winters stack snow, thaw it, refreeze it and add more. Wet spring snow and ice layers can weigh several times as much as fresh powder at the same depth. The Metrodome’s fabric roof deflating under heavy snow in December 2010 is the best-known example, but most snow-load claims involve garages, porches, pole buildings and flat commercial roofs.</p>
<h2>How the estimate is calculated</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Snow</strong>: depth in feet × typical density for the snow type you choose.</li>
<li><strong>Ice</strong>: thickness in feet × about 57 lb/ft³.</li>
<li><strong>Total</strong>: the sum, compared with the reference load you select, and multiplied by roof area if you enter one.</li>
</ul>
<p>Densities vary widely in real snowpacks; these are rough planning values.</p>
<h2>Snow load, ice dams and your insurance claim</h2>
<p>Collapse from the weight of snow or ice is a covered peril in many homeowner forms, while gradual deterioration is not. Ice dams that push water under shingles are a separate issue covered on our <a href="/appraisal-solutions/ice-dam-winter-damage-appraisal/">ice dam &amp; winter damage</a> page. If a roof is sagging, cracking or leaking, get people out and call a qualified contractor or structural engineer — don’t climb on it.</p>
<p>This calculator is not an engineering evaluation. Reference loads shown are examples, not your building’s rated capacity.</p>
<h2>Questions about this tool</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How much does a foot of snow weigh on a roof?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on density. Fresh powder might be around 5–7 pounds per square foot per foot of depth, while wet or packed snow can be 20 pounds or more.</p></div></details><details><summary>What is my roof designed to hold?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Design snow loads depend on building code, location, roof shape and age. Building plans or a structural engineer can tell you; this tool only offers example reference values.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is roof collapse from snow covered by insurance?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many homeowner policies cover collapse caused by the weight of snow or ice, but terms and exclusions vary. Read your policy’s collapse provisions.</p></div></details></div><h2>More free tools</h2><div class="related"><a href="/free-tools/claim-gap-fee-estimator/"><small>Calculator</small><span>Claim Gap &amp; Fee Estimator</span><svg class="i" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/free-tools/rcv-acv-holdback-calculator/"><small>Calculat